# =============================================================================
#  .dockerignore  --  requirement 2.9 (no confidential data in the build)
# =============================================================================
#  The build context is everything the daemon can see. `COPY . /app` copies all
#  of it, and files you never named end up in a layer that ships worldwide:
#  .git (with its full history), .env, kubeconfig, ~/.aws that got copied in for
#  a test, terraform.tfstate with its plaintext values, id_rsa.
#
#  This file is a DENY LIST, which means it fails open: anything you forget to
#  list is included. That is backwards for a security control. Prefer an
#  allow-list posture in the Dockerfile itself - copy named files
#  (`COPY app/go.mod ./`) rather than `COPY . .` - and treat .dockerignore as
#  the backstop. The hardened Dockerfile in this directory does exactly that.
# =============================================================================

# Version control - carries every secret ever committed, including "deleted" ones
.git
.gitignore
.github

# Local environment and credentials
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
id_rsa
id_ed25519
.netrc
.npmrc
.docker/config.json
.aws/
.kube/
kubeconfig

# Infrastructure state - Terraform state files store secrets in plaintext
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.backup
.terraform/

# Build output and scan results
results/
.tools/
*.log

# Editor and OS noise
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Documentation - no reason for it to be in the image
*.md
docs/
